import{_ as s,E as n,h as o,c as r,b as a,f as i,H as p,a4 as d}from"./chunks/framework.B_fYP6Of.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"From App Studio to your hosted RAG","description":"Extend the FastAPI app you already deployed. Add import, real retrieval, a model response, and a source link, then test the same path on your server.","frontmatter":{"title":"From App Studio to your hosted RAG","description":"Extend the FastAPI app you already deployed. Add import, real retrieval, a model response, and a source link, then test the same path on your server.","teaser":"You already have a server and an app. Follow one document through the new feature while keeping the accounts, routes, and deployment that work."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"students/from-app-studio.md","filePath":"students/from-app-studio.md"}'),c={name:"students/from-app-studio.md"};function l(h,e,u,m,g,f){const t=n("LabArticleHeader");return o(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=a("h1",{id:"from-your-existing-fastapi-app-to-a-small-hosted-rag",tabindex:"-1"},[i("From your existing FastAPI app to a small hosted RAG "),a("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#from-your-existing-fastapi-app-to-a-small-hosted-rag","aria-label":'Permalink to "From your existing FastAPI app to a small hosted RAG"'},"​")],-1)),p(t),e[1]||(e[1]=d(`<p>Open a URL your deployed app already serves. Find the FastAPI route for that URL in your repository and point to the function that handled the request. If you cannot match them, compare the deployed revision with your checkout before adding retrieval.</p><p>Work in that same repository. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/is373-app-studio" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">App Studio</a> supplied the FastAPI app, accounts, and permissions. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/373_hosting" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Server Studio</a> supplied the Docker, Traefik, and HTTPS setup. This optional walkthrough adds a single feature to that foundation.</p><p>Choose a permitted document, a question it answers, and a model you can access. Your finished path needs real retrieval and generation. If those pieces are unfamiliar, try the <a href="./reference">local retrieval lab</a>, <a href="./embeddings">embeddings</a>, or <a href="./chunking">chunking</a>. Add a <a href="./library-survey">library</a> only when you can name the job you need it to do.</p><h2 id="picture-the-smallest-complete-product" tabindex="-1">Picture the smallest complete product <a class="header-anchor" href="#picture-the-smallest-complete-product" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Picture the smallest complete product&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Five permitted source documents</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → importer → source IDs + chunks (or sourced graph relationships)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → persistent index</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → question in YOUR FastAPI app</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → real retrieval: source IDs and passages/paths</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → real model call with selected evidence</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  → answer and openable source references</span></span></code></pre></div><p>If you choose vectors, use <strong>real compatible embeddings</strong> for both corpus and query. If you choose a graph, trace real relationships to original passages. You must research both approaches but implement only the one(s) you justify.</p><h2 id="checkpoint-1-—-understand-your-own-starting-point" tabindex="-1">Checkpoint 1 — Understand your own starting point <a class="header-anchor" href="#checkpoint-1-—-understand-your-own-starting-point" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Checkpoint 1 — Understand your own starting point&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Open <strong>your student repository</strong>, not the instructor&#39;s template. Locate <code>app/main.py</code> and <code>create_app()</code>. Find the existing feature routers (<code>app/features/</code>), persistence/configuration, templates and tests. Identify your actual server URL and working Docker/HTTPS deployment. Don&#39;t alter accounts, roles, domains or unrelated services.</p><p><strong>Check your starting point.</strong> Find one existing route, the function that registers it, the code that writes to the database, and the settings that hold secrets. If a part is unclear, bring that file and one question to class.</p><h2 id="checkpoint-2-—-trace-one-original-source" tabindex="-1">Checkpoint 2 — Trace one original source <a class="header-anchor" href="#checkpoint-2-—-trace-one-original-source" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Checkpoint 2 — Trace one original source&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Choose one permitted short document and one useful question. Give the source a stable ID, title, origin, version/date and permission note. Prepare a chunk with a source location, or a graph entity/relationship that points back to an actual passage. First inspect this with the <a href="./reference#your-first-15-minutes">15-minute offline lab</a> if that helps.</p><p><strong>Try it twice.</strong> Import the same document again and compare the stored IDs and counts. Then edit a copy, import the new version, and check that retrieval can find the changed passage. Keep any unrun checks marked as planned.</p><h2 id="checkpoint-3-—-add-one-narrow-retrieval-feature" tabindex="-1">Checkpoint 3 — Add one narrow retrieval feature <a class="header-anchor" href="#checkpoint-3-—-add-one-narrow-retrieval-feature" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Checkpoint 3 — Add one narrow retrieval feature&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Ask your AI coding partner to help add a <strong>small feature router</strong> to your existing FastAPI app (for example, <code>app/features/rag.py</code>), with one <code>POST /api/rag/ask</code> route and separate import/retrieval/generation functions. These are example names, <strong>not</strong> required file or endpoint names.</p><p>Give ordinary visitors access to the question flow. Restrict import to an authorized account or a trusted command-line tool. Have the retrieval stage return evidence IDs in test or debug output. Keyword search gives you a baseline, then implement the vector or graph retrieval you chose.</p><p><strong>Check the feature in isolation.</strong> Ask a supported question and inspect the actual IDs, passages, or graph paths returned. Try a question with no supporting passage. Run the existing App Studio checks, including APP-001–003, along with focused checks for the new feature.</p><h2 id="checkpoint-4-—-generate-a-grounded-answer" tabindex="-1">Checkpoint 4 — Generate a grounded answer <a class="header-anchor" href="#checkpoint-4-—-generate-a-grounded-answer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Checkpoint 4 — Generate a grounded answer&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Use a model/provider you can actually access. Send only relevant <strong>authorized</strong> retrieved evidence to the model through server-side configuration. Keep provider credentials out of JavaScript, commits, screenshots and logs.</p><p>Display both the answer <strong>and independently openable references</strong>. Ask the model to abstain when evidence is missing, but don&#39;t trust the prompt alone: test an unanswerable question, inspect output and explain what remains uncertain.</p><p><strong>Check:</strong> Save the actual retrieval results, the redacted context supplied to the model, the actual model response, and which claims the cited sources support. Canned text is a UI mock, not completed RAG.</p><h2 id="checkpoint-5-—-deploy-the-same-app-you-tested" tabindex="-1">Checkpoint 5 — Deploy the same app you tested <a class="header-anchor" href="#checkpoint-5-—-deploy-the-same-app-you-tested" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Checkpoint 5 — Deploy the same app you tested&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Reuse the existing student server and Docker/Traefik/HTTPS setup. Put persistent data in an appropriate Docker volume or other durable store. Keep database services private, restrict administrative routes, and bound usage/cost for a public demo. <strong>Don&#39;t change DNS or discard a working calculator service without checking first.</strong></p><p><strong>Check:</strong> Open the HTTPS app as a visitor, ask a supported and unsupported question, click a cited source, then restart the app and confirm the stored sources remain. Preserve the exact working source revision and a safe deployment procedure.</p><h2 id="what-to-show-in-class" tabindex="-1">What to show in class <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-to-show-in-class" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What to show in class&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For <strong>Plan</strong>, bring the source plan, vector/graph comparison, sketch, and expected evidence. For <strong>Build</strong>, show a small hosted journey from import through retrieval to generation. At <strong>Checked Release</strong>, demonstrate persistence, usable citations, repeatable setup, and evaluation. For <strong>Improve</strong>, act on a real peer observation and retest. Use Canvas for deadlines and demo arrangements.</p><h2 id="copyable-ai-coding-agent-request" tabindex="-1">Copyable AI coding-agent request <a class="header-anchor" href="#copyable-ai-coding-agent-request" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Copyable AI coding-agent request&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>I&#39;m extending MY existing IS373 App Studio repo for the individual RAG</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>assignment. Read README.md, AGENTS.md, SPEC.md, app/main.py and the current</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>routers/settings/tests. First tell me what already works and ask me to</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>confirm my permitted corpus and model access. Do not edit yet.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>I need ONE small import → retrieval → model answer path with traceable</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>sources, using my existing FastAPI, auth, persistence and server setup.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Research vector and graph but implement only the justified choice.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Propose the next smallest issue, a 3–5-step plan and observable checks.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>When I approve, make a bounded change and run REAL tests; don&#39;t claim a</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>mock is generation or a keyword baseline is a vector/graph system.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Never reset my app, server, database, DNS or permissions to make progress.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Show me code paths, actual outputs and one question to check my understanding.</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/is373-studio/blob/main/docs/projects/individual.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">original assignment</a> is the grading authority. The goal is a result students can <strong>understand, defend and improve</strong>, not the largest framework.</p><p><a href="./">Back to student guide</a> · <a href="./library-survey">Library survey</a> · <a href="./prompts">AI coding prompts</a> · <a href="./check">Five evaluation questions</a></p>`,29))])}const v=s(c,[["render",l]]);export{k as __pageData,v as default};
